WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:03.840
Hello there! It’s finally here: the long-promised&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:08.880
video explaining relative positional&nbsp;
embeddings as they were introduced in&nbsp;

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:14.240
prehistoric times by our&nbsp;
ancestors in machine learning.

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:18.800
So, if you are not yet sick of positional&nbsp;
embeddings in transformers and want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:23.520
more of them, you are spending your&nbsp;
coffee break with the right video!

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:29.200
Because well…, we did a video about positional&nbsp;
embeddings as introduced by the “attention is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:35.760
all you need” paper, then we had a video about&nbsp;
concatenating versus adding positional encodings.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:39.200
And there, we also discussed&nbsp;
learned positional embeddings.

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:44.880
Ms. Coffee Bean is now literally 50%&nbsp;
caffeine and 50% positional information!

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:50.240
Let’s begin: The “attention is all you need” paper
introduces positional embeddings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:56.160
that encode the absolute position.
They encode the exact order of tokens,&nbsp;

00:00:56.160 --> 00:01:01.120
such that the transformer is informed&nbsp;
about the sequential nature of data.&nbsp;

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:04.720
Otherwise, the transformer that is&nbsp;
processing everything in parallel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:07.440
would be invariant to order, meaning that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:14.320
without positional encodings, its output would&nbsp;
not change after complete sequence reordering.

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:20.400
But what if we are dealing with other kinds of&nbsp;
data where it is not about absolute position,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:25.440
but about relative positions?&nbsp;
Think about a graph, for example.&nbsp;

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:30.240
It would be quite arbitrary to say&nbsp;
that a certain node is the first one.&nbsp;

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:33.760
What would make this node the first node and
this the second?&nbsp;

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:39.840
That would be unmotivated for many problems&nbsp;
and in some cases, even misleading.

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:45.040
Then let’s forget about absolute&nbsp;
order and move to relative positional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:50.400
encodings that are about the distances&nbsp;
between elements in either a graph,&nbsp;

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:55.600
or a sequence -- which is in&nbsp;
fact a degenerate graph, a chain.

00:01:56.240 --> 00:02:00.560
A subset of the authors of the “attention is&nbsp;
all you need” paper, also have a follow-up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:07.600
paper doing exactly this: they introduce relative&nbsp;
positional encodings where not the order, but the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:14.240
relation or better said: the relative position,&nbsp;
the distance between tokens is important.

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:19.040
So how does this work? If you do not know&nbsp;
anything about positional embeddings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:24.800
first go and watch our two videos about them&nbsp;
and then come back. Don’t worry, they’re short.

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:31.200
The idea of relative embeddings is moving away&nbsp;
from the classical positional embeddings where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:34.240
each token has its own positional embedding.

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:40.640
With relative representations, each word or token&nbsp;
does not have only one positional embedding,&nbsp;

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:45.440
but as many positional embeddings&nbsp;
as there are tokens in the sequence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:48.800
in order to describe the&nbsp;
relationship between them.

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:53.840
This is because relative positional&nbsp;
representations do not encode absolute&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.840 --> 00:03:00.880
order anymore, but the positional relationship&nbsp;
in which each token stands to the other tokens.

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:08.640
So, let’s take an example sequence of 5 tokens.
In classical positional embeddings, each token --&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:15.680
let’s say x_4 here -- has one encoding informing&nbsp;
the transformer about the position of x_4.

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:22.160
Now, to best visualize relative representations,&nbsp;
let’s copy the sequence again like this. In the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:26.240
relative variant, each token&nbsp;
has 5 positional embeddings.&nbsp;

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:30.560
One embedding for describing&nbsp;
its relative position to itself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:36.000
then 4 others for the rest of the sequence.
And to keep the same notation as the original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:39.200
paper, the positional embedding&nbsp;
describing the relationship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:47.520
of one token with itself is a vector w_0,
as 0 is the distance between the token and itself.&nbsp;

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:57.280
Then w_1, w_2 as we move to the right and w_-1&nbsp;
and w_-2 and so on, as we move to the left.

00:03:57.280 --> 00:04:01.440
And the vector w_i takes the&nbsp;
same value, independently of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:07.120
what token we are currently looking at,
because w_0 -- wherever we are -- says&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:13.600
that we are 0 hops away in the graph.
w_1 says that we are one hop away to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:21.680
the left in a sequence; or in a directed graph in&nbsp;
general, we go one hop following outgoing edges.&nbsp;

00:04:21.680 --> 00:04:21.920
W_-1 means that we go one&nbsp;
hop with the ingoing edges.

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:26.480
So you see, this approach is not made&nbsp;
only for sequences, but for graphs too.&nbsp;

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:29.600
Ok, great. For a sequence of length five,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:34.880
positional embeddings should&nbsp;
range from w_-4 to w_4,&nbsp;

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:41.600
so we have in total 9 positional embeddings to&nbsp;
either handcraft or learn for this sequence.&nbsp;

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:46.640
These w vectors can be written one&nbsp;
under the other in a table like this.

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:51.680
Notice, that these vectors come from&nbsp;
the relationship or distance between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:56.560
one token and the other so we can&nbsp;
use a pairwise notation for this.

00:04:56.560 --> 00:05:02.160
The authors also experiment with clipping&nbsp;
at k, meaning that after a certain distance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:12.080
positional embeddings get the same value.
So for k=2: w_3 and w_4 all take the value of w_2.

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:17.360
Okay, but now for each token, we are stuck&nbsp;
with as many positional embeddings as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.360 --> 00:05:24.160
sequence has tokens, what to do with all of these?
Add them all up? That wouldn’t be a great idea:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:30.480
addition is complicating things even with&nbsp;
sinusoidal embeddings where we have just one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:36.160
positional encoding for each token because
we could mix up the semantic and positional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.160 --> 00:05:39.760
information. With 5 positional&nbsp;
embeddings per token, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.760 --> 00:05:48.000
mixing-up problem would be only 5 times larger.
Hm… if only we had a mechanism that given a token,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:54.320
computes new representations for that token&nbsp;
in relationship to all other tokens ...

00:05:54.320 --> 00:06:00.640
Well, we do have this, it’s the self-attention&nbsp;
mechanism! A clever idea would be to modify&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.640 --> 00:06:05.760
the self-attention formulas to capture the&nbsp;
relative positional representations too.

00:06:05.760 --> 00:06:11.200
We remember that through self-attention,&nbsp;
each token gets a new representation z&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:14.320
which is passed further in the transformer module.

00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:19.280
Each token vector is first transformed&nbsp;
by a linear transformation.

00:06:19.280 --> 00:06:26.720
Then the new representation for each token is a&nbsp;
weighted sum over all tokens, where the weights&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.720 --> 00:06:33.760
are sort-of an importance score. So fellow&nbsp;
tokens that matter more are weighted more.

00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:38.880
So here, where the new representations&nbsp;
are computed, we can add the positional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:43.840
information! Now, the token representation&nbsp;
after the linear transformation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.840 --> 00:06:51.200
is further shifted in the high-dimensional space.
This means that each token x_j gets pushed into a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.200 --> 00:06:58.240
subspace saying that "look, I carry this semantic&nbsp;
information, but I am also your 2nd neighbor to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.240 --> 00:07:05.040
your right because my position in some dimension&nbsp;
is similar to all other 2nd neighbors, ok?"

00:07:05.920 --> 00:07:11.120
Great, now the new representation z is&nbsp;
informed about the relative position,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.120 --> 00:07:14.160
but the self-attention&nbsp;
weight coefficients are not.&nbsp;

00:07:14.720 --> 00:07:21.280
Therefore, the weight coefficients also receive&nbsp;
their own positional push such that the importance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.280 --> 00:07:27.840
scores make position-informed decisions too.
So, what do we have in the end?&nbsp;

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:35.040
Now every token has as many relative embeddings as&nbsp;
there are tokens in the sequence in two variants:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.600 --> 00:07:41.760
one relative embedding for the values and one&nbsp;
for the keys to inform the attention weights.

00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:47.520
And the exact values of the vectors are learned,&nbsp;
which kind-of makes sense to let the model figure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:53.280
out for itself what the best balance is. It&nbsp;
would be quite difficult to hand-craft these.

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:55.920
And what did this whole thing help with?

00:07:56.640 --> 00:08:01.760
Well, this paper has only experimented&nbsp;
with text, which is a sequence obviously,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.760 --> 00:08:05.200
and they gain some performance&nbsp;
in machine translation.

00:08:05.200 --> 00:08:12.640
But word chains are only a flat graph and&nbsp;
beyond this, the method can be applied to graph&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.640 --> 00:08:18.640
representations in general or anywhere where you&nbsp;
have pairwise relationships between your elements.

00:08:18.640 --> 00:08:24.400
Because remember, these relative representations&nbsp;
depend only on how far tokens are from one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.400 --> 00:08:30.000
another, independently of whether we are&nbsp;
in a sequence or in a more complex graph.

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:33.680
But Ms. Coffee Bean, why didn’t&nbsp;
the paper try this out immediately?&nbsp;

00:08:34.640 --> 00:08:37.120
Well, it’s called "marking your territory".&nbsp;

00:08:37.120 --> 00:08:41.440
But there are other papers that implemented&nbsp;
this successfully for graphs too.

00:08:41.440 --> 00:08:45.280
Anyway, another upside of relative&nbsp;
positional representations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.280 --> 00:08:48.080
is that especially with clipping at k,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.800 --> 00:08:55.840
this learned positional information generalizes&nbsp;
to any sequence length, which is also a feature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.840 --> 00:09:02.640
of the hand-crafted sinusoidal embeddings, but not&nbsp;
necessarily of any learned positional embedding.

00:09:02.640 --> 00:09:07.680
Clipping at k seems a little odd&nbsp;
because with small k, it takes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:14.640
away the information of long-term dependencies.
Now we can only store that something is close by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.640 --> 00:09:18.560
or far away, but we don't&nbsp;
know how far away that is.

00:09:18.560 --> 00:09:22.000
But this is similar with Graph Neural&nbsp;
Networks where the best results&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:25.280
are delivered with a small number of iterations.

00:09:25.280 --> 00:09:31.440
Many iterations allow for information from&nbsp;
further away to diffuse to your point of interest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.440 --> 00:09:38.000
but multiple hops are not always beneficial as&nbsp;
it can aggregate noisy signals from far away.

00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:43.920
So clipping at k in relative positional&nbsp;
representations acts a little like a relative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.920 --> 00:09:51.360
position event horizon which does not hurt&nbsp;
tasks which mostly rely on local dependencies.

00:09:51.360 --> 00:09:55.920
Well, we hope that these relative&nbsp;
embeddings and the associated paper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.920 --> 00:09:58.160
are now more digestible to you.

00:09:58.160 --> 00:10:02.720
Thanks for staying until the end of this&nbsp;
explanation and if you’re still here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.720 --> 00:10:05.680
do not forget to leave a like and subscribe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.680 --> 00:10:17.585
you know, to help with the YouTube algorithm.
Okay, bye!

